        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Analyse User Question"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1771580404931"
          },
          "code": "ANALYSE_USER_QUESTION",
          "id": "1771573406375",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1771573406378_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Analyse the user’s question and determine the exact search scope required to answer it correctly, and whether to call any API or not.\n\n## User Question:\n {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}} \n\n## Decision Rules (must follow strictly):\n- Choose the smallest sufficient search scope that can fully answer the question.\n- If answering the question requires multiple steps, choose the search mode that reflects the required execution order.\n- If the question is ambiguous, prefer the broader search over a narrower one.\n- Never guess. Base the decision only on what is required to answer the question.\n\n## Search Mode Definitions (choose exactly one):\n- SINGLE_CONTRACT → The answer can be found within one contract only.\n- ACROSS_CONTRACTS → The answer requires searching across multiple contracts.\n- ACROSS_CLAUSES → The answer requires searching across clauses in the standard clause library.\n- ACROSS_CONTRACTS_CLAUSES → The answer requires searching across both contracts and clauses.\n\n## callAPI\nSet `callAPI` to \"true\" if:\n- The question refers to standard/template/library clauses\n- The question requires clause library or multiple contracts\n- The question refers to a specific supplier, party, or contract not explicitly provided in Contract Document Text\n- The required source is not fully available in the input\n\nSet `callAPI` to \"false\" ONLY if:\n- The question is strictly about the current contract\n- AND explicitly refers to this contract\n- AND Contract Document Text fully contains the answer\n\nContract Document Text - {{$context.$variables.ContractDocumentText}}\n\n## Output Constraints (mandatory):\n- Do not add explanations, text, or formatting.\n- Do not invent new categories.\n\n## Output:\nSearchMode\ncallAPI"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n       \"SearchMode\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n       \"callAPI\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n   },\n   \"required\": [\n        \"SearchMode\", \"callAPI\"\n   ]\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Prepare 1B Response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "PREPARE_1B_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1771578615655",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1771578615658_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "- Fetch <ThirdPartyClause> from {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\n- Extract upto 5 clauses from {{$context.$nodes.CONTRACTS_CI_1B.$output}} that are semantically similar or substantially aligned with <ThirdPartyClause>, considering obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, limitations, and legal intent — not just keyword overlap.\n\n- For EACH extracted clause:\n  - Compare <ThirdPartyClause> with <extracted clause text> strictly based on the provided text only. Identify semantic deviations in obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, or limitations. Present concise bullet points totaling under 100 words. Do not infer, assume, or add information not present in the clauses.\n  - Generate one JSON object per clause using the exact structure defined below.\n\n- Relevance filtering:\n  - For each extracted clause, first determine its primary purpose in one sentence.\n  - Select the clause ONLY if that primary purpose matches the primary purpose of <ThirdPartyClause>.\n  - Overlap in wording or shared topics alone does NOT qualify.\n  - If the match cannot be clearly justified based on purpose, exclude the clause.\n  - Exclude the clauses where semantic similarity is very low.\n  - Select upto 5 clauses.\n  - Do NOT force selection.\n\n## Clause Extraction Instruction\nFor EACH <extracted clause text>:\n- Ignore/remove any numbering or section labels anywhere in the heading (e.g., Article, Section, 1, 1.2, 9.1).\n- If multiple headings appear at the start, always choose the most specific (lowest-level) heading, meaning the heading closest to the body sentence.\n- Parent/section headings must NOT be used as <ClauseName>.\n- If no clear heading exists, use an empty string.\n- Everything after the <ClauseName> is <ClauseText>. Normalize whitespace and keep wording unchanged.\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT ##\n- When generating your output, always replace technical variable names with user-friendly explanations: \n    - Refer to ThirdPartyClause as \"the document clause\"\n    - Refer to <extracted clause text> as \"the clause from prior contract\"\n- You MUST return ONLY valid JSON.\n- Each object corresponds to one clause comparison.\n- All values MUST be strings.\n- If no similar clauses are found, return an empty array: [].\n- Do NOT include explanations, markdown, code fences, comments, or extra text.\n- Do NOT add, remove, or rename fields.\n- Do NOT change field casing.\n- Do NOT include trailing commas.\n\n- The output MUST be a JSON array containing BETWEEN 0 AND 5 objects.\n- The array MUST NOT contain more than 5 objects.\n\n[\n  {\n    \"ClauseFromContract\": {\n      \"ContractNumber\": \"<CONTRACT_NUMBER>\",\n      \"ClauseName\": \"<ClauseName>\",\n      \"ClauseText\": \"<ClauseText>\"\n    },\n    \"Deviation\": [\"<bullet 1>\", \"<bullet 2>\", \"<bullet 3>\"]\n  }\n]"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Prepare 1C Response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "PREPARE_1C_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1771579768602",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1771579768605_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "- Fetch <ThirdPartyClause> from {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\n- Extract upto 5 clauses from {{$context.$nodes.CONTRACTS_CI_1C.$output}} that are semantically similar or substantially aligned with <ThirdPartyClause>, considering obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, limitations, and legal intent — not just keyword overlap.\n\n- For EACH extracted clause:\n  - Compare <ThirdPartyClause> with <extracted clause text> strictly based on the provided text only. Identify semantic deviations in obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, or limitations. Present concise bullet points totaling under 100 words. Do not infer, assume, or add information not present in the clauses.\n  - Generate one JSON object per clause using the exact structure defined below.\n\n- Relevance filtering:\n  - For each extracted clause, first determine its primary purpose in one sentence.\n  - Select the clause ONLY if that primary purpose matches the primary purpose of <ThirdPartyClause>.\n  - Overlap in wording or shared topics alone does NOT qualify.\n  - If the match cannot be clearly justified based on purpose, exclude the clause.\n  - Exclude the clauses where semantic similarity is very low.\n  - Select upto 5 clauses.\n  - Do NOT force selection.\n\n## Clause Name & Text Extraction Instruction\nFor EACH <extracted clause text>:\n- Ignore/remove any numbering or section labels anywhere in the heading (e.g., Article, Section, 1, 1.2, 9.1).\n- If multiple headings appear at the start, always choose the most specific (lowest-level) heading, meaning the heading closest to the body sentence.\n- Parent/section headings must NOT be used as <ClauseName>.\n- If no clear heading exists, use an empty string.\n- Everything after the <ClauseName> is <ClauseText>. Normalize whitespace and keep wording unchanged.\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT ##\n- When generating your output, always replace technical variable names with user-friendly explanations: \n    - Refer to ThirdPartyClause as \"the document clause\"\n    - Refer to <extracted clause text> as \"the clause from prior contract\"\n- You MUST return ONLY valid JSON.\n- Each object corresponds to one clause comparison.\n- All values MUST be strings.\n- If no similar clauses are found, return an empty array: [].\n- Do NOT include explanations, markdown, code fences, comments, or extra text.\n- Do NOT add, remove, or rename fields.\n- Do NOT change field casing.\n- Do NOT include trailing commas.\n\n- The output MUST be a JSON array containing BETWEEN 0 AND 5 objects.\n- The array MUST NOT contain more than 5 objects.\n\n[\n  {\n    \"ClauseFromContract\": {\n      \"ContractNumber\": \"<CONTRACT_NUMBER>\",\n      \"PartyName\": \"<PRIMARY_PARTY_NAME>\",\n      \"ClauseName\": \"<ClauseName>\",\n      \"ClauseText\": \"<ClauseText>\"\n    },\n    \"Deviation\": [\"<bullet 1>\", \"<bullet 2>\", \"<bullet 3>\"]\n  }\n]"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Prepare 1A Response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "PREPARE_1A_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1771579986840",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1771579986841_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "- Fetch <ThirdPartyClause>, <ClauseVersionId> from {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}.\n- Fetch <CLAUSE_TEXT> from {{$context.$nodes.CLAUSE_CI_1A.$output}} \n\n- Compare <ThirdPartyClause> with <CLAUSE_TEXT> strictly based on the provided text only. Identify semantic deviations in obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, or limitations. Present concise bullet points totaling under 100 words. Do not infer, assume, or add information not present in the clauses.\n\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT ##\n\nProvide the final output in JSON format.\n{\n   \"Deviation\": [\"<bullet 1>\", \"<bullet 2>\", \"<bullet 3>\"],\n   \"StandardClause\":<CLAUSE_TEXT>,\n   \"ArticleVersionId\":<ClauseVersionId>\n}\n\n- When generating your output, always replace technical variable names with user-friendly explanations: \n    - Refer to ThirdPartyClause as \"the document clause\"\n    - Refer to CLAUSE_TEXT as \"the standard clause\"\n- You MUST return ONLY valid JSON.\n- If no similar clauses are found, return an empty array: [].\n- Do NOT include explanations, markdown, code fences, comments, or extra text.\n- Do NOT add, remove, or rename fields.\n- Do NOT change field casing.\n- Do NOT include trailing commas."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Prepare 1A1 Response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "PREPARE_1A1_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1771580059215",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1771580059217_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "- Fetch <ThirdPartyClause> from {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}.\n\n- From {{$context.$nodes.CLAUSE_CI_1A1.$output}}:\n  - From each clause object, extract ONLY the following fields:\n    - CLAUSE_NUMBER\n    - CLAUSE_NAME\n    - CLAUSE_TEXT\n    - CLAUSE_ID\n    - CLAUSE_VERSION_ID\n\n- Using ONLY the extracted CLAUSE_TEXT values:\n  - Find clauses whose CLAUSE_TEXT is semantically similar or substantially aligned with <ThirdPartyClause>, considering obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, limitations, and legal intent — not just keyword overlap.\n  - Rank clauses by similarity (highest to lowest).\n\n- Relevance filtering:\n  - For each clause, first determine its primary purpose in one sentence.\n  - Select the clause ONLY if that primary purpose matches the primary purpose of <ThirdPartyClause>.\n  - Overlap in wording or shared topics alone does NOT qualify.\n  - If the match cannot be clearly justified based on purpose, exclude the clause.\n  - Exclude the clauses where semantic similarity is very low.\n  - Select UP TO 5 clauses.\n  - If NO clauses qualify, return an empty array: [].\n  - Do NOT force selection.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Semantic similarity MUST be based ONLY on CLAUSE_TEXT.\n- Do NOT use ClauseName, metadata, IDs, or any other fields for similarity.\n- Do NOT force selection.\n\n- For EACH selected clause:\n  - Compare <ThirdPartyClause> with <CLAUSE_TEXT> strictly based on the provided text only. Identify semantic deviations in obligations, rights, scope, risk allocation, exclusions, or limitations. Present concise bullet points totaling under 100 words. Do not infer, assume, or add information not present in the clauses.\n  - Produce ONE JSON object per clause using the exact structure below.\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT ##\n- When generating your output, always replace technical variable names with user-friendly explanations: \n    - Refer to ThirdPartyClause as \"the document clause\"\n    - Refer to CLAUSE_TEXT as \"the standard clause\"\n- You MUST return ONLY valid JSON.\n- Do NOT include explanations, markdown, code fences, comments, or extra text.\n- Do NOT add, remove, or rename fields.\n- Do NOT change field casing.\n- Do NOT include trailing commas.\n\nThe output MUST be a JSON array containing BETWEEN 0 AND 5 objects.\nThe array MUST NOT contain more than 5 objects.\n\n[\n  {\n    \"StdClauseFromLib\": {\n      \"ClauseNumber\": \"<CLAUSE_NUMBER>\",\n      \"ClauseName\": \"<TITLE>\",\n      \"ClauseText\": \"<CLAUSE_TEXT>\",\n      \"ClauseId\": \"<CLAUSE_ID>\",\n      \"ClauseVersionId\": \"<CLAUSE_VERSION_ID>\"\n    },\n    \"Deviation\": [\"<bullet 1>\", \"<bullet 2>\", \"<bullet 3>\"]\n  }\n]\n\nRules:\n- Each object corresponds to exactly one clause comparison.\n- All values MUST be strings.\n- If no similar clauses are found, return an empty array: [].\n- Output MUST be valid JSON. Any response that violates these rules is invalid."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
